    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-the-bald"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald.jpg" alt="Charles the Bald" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale_de_France_-_Bible_de_Vivien_Ms._Latin_1_folio_423r_d%C3%A9tail_Le_comte_Vivien_offre_le_manuscrit_de_la_Bible_faite_%C3%A0_l%27abbaye_de_Saint-Martin_de_Tours_%C3%A0_Charles_le_Chauve.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Comte Vivien - Moines de l'abbaye Saint-Martin de Tours, vers 850, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a> began with a scandal. In 862 Baldwin, a Frankish noble later called Iron Arm, eloped with Judith, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> and already the widow of two kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wessex">Wessex</a>. After the couple sought the protection of the pope, Charles was reconciled with them and gave Baldwin lands around Bruges in the coastal district called Flanders, which he was to defend against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikings">Viking</a> raids. His son Baldwin II (879–918), who married Ælfthryth, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, used the disorder of the Viking age to build fortresses and gather counties, abbeys and royal lands into a single principality. Arnulf I (918–965) extended it southwards to Artois and the region of Arras. By the eleventh century the counts ruled a territory from the Scheldt to the Canche, most of it held from the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, together with lands east of the Scheldt, known as Imperial Flanders, held from the German emperors. Baldwin V (1035–1067) was among the most powerful princes in France: he served as regent for the young Philip I from 1060, and his daughter Matilda married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>. The county’s heartland was the Flemish-speaking north, but it also included French-speaking districts around Lille, Douai and Arras.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#antwerp"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antwerp.jpg" alt="Antwerp" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#antwerp">Antwerp</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amberes;_vistas_MAS_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LBM1948, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a> became one of the most densely populated and urbanised regions of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Land reclaimed from the sea and the coastal marshes supported large flocks of sheep, and from the eleventh century the towns of Ghent, Bruges, Ypres, Arras, Douai and Lille specialised in high-quality woollen cloth, increasingly made from imported English wool. This dependence tied Flanders economically to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, while politically it owed allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>. Ghent, with perhaps 60,000 inhabitants in the fourteenth century, was one of the largest cities north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alps">Alps</a>. Bruges, linked to the sea by the Zwin inlet and its outport of Damme, became the leading commercial centre of northern Europe, where Italian bankers, Hanseatic merchants and traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, England and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scotland">Scotland</a> met; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> kept a <em>kontor</em> there, and the square before the house of the Van der Beurse family gave the word bourse to the language of finance. Cloth workers organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guild">guilds</a> formed a large urban workforce whose wages and politics often brought violent conflict with the patrician elite. In the late fourteenth and fifteenth centuries the Flemish cloth industry declined under English competition, and the silting of the Zwin moved trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antwerp">Antwerp</a>, but Flanders turned to lighter cloths, linen and tapestry and remained one of the richest regions of Europe.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#first-crusade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-crusade.jpg" alt="First Crusade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#first-crusade">First Crusade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_the_Hermit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a> was rarely a great military power in its own right, but its wealth and strategic position made it a battlefield. Count Robert I the Frisian won the county at the Battle of Cassel in 1071, and Flemish knights were prominent on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-crusade">First Crusade</a> under Count Robert II. Count Philip of Alsace died at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acre">Acre</a> in 1191 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>, and Baldwin IX was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204. The French crown’s efforts to control Flanders led to repeated wars. Count Ferrand was captured at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214, fighting in the coalition of Otto IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> against Philip II Augustus. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> occupied the county in 1300, the townsmen of Bruges rose in the Bruges Matins of May 1302, and on 11 July 1302 an army of Flemish militias, fighting on foot with pikes and the spiked club called the <em>goedendag</em>, destroyed the French cavalry at the Battle of the Golden Spurs near Courtrai. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> took revenge at Mons-en-Pévèle in 1304, Cassel in 1328 and Roosebeke in 1382. In the Hundred Years’ War the English fleet destroyed the French navy at the Battle of Sluys off the Flemish coast in 1340, and in later centuries southern Flanders was repeatedly fought over by France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-bold"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold.jpg" alt="Philip the Bold" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16th-century_unknown_painters_-_Philip_the_Bold_-_WGA23677.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a> built one of the most effective principalities of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. They administered their lands through castellanies, each centred on a fortress and run by a castellan, and from the twelfth century increasingly through salaried bailiffs who could be dismissed. Comital revenues were audited centrally, and the account of 1187 known as the <em>Grote Brief</em> is among the earliest surviving princely financial records of its kind in Europe. The murder of Count Charles the Good in the church of St Donatian at Bruges in 1127, described in detail by the notary Galbert of Bruges, set off a succession crisis in which the towns for the first time claimed a say in choosing the count. The great towns enjoyed wide self-government under aldermen called <em>schepenen</em>, and by the fourteenth century Ghent, Bruges and Ypres, joined later by the rural district of the Franc of Bruges, formed the Four Members of Flanders, which negotiated taxation with the count. From 1338 to 1345 Jacob van Artevelde of Ghent in effect governed much of the county in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>. The Burgundian dukes added a Council of Flanders, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> in 1386, as the county’s high court. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> Flanders kept its own estates and law, and French suzerainty over it was renounced in 1526 and 1529.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Charles_V,_Holy_Roman_Emperor,_seated_(1500%E2%80%931558),_formerly_attributed_to_Titian_(Alte_Pinakothek,_Munich).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a>’ wealth gave it an influence far beyond its size. Its cloth industry made it, with northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>, one of the two great manufacturing centres of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, and its towns pioneered forms of commercial life, from fairs and exchanges to urban self-government, that spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#low-countries">Low Countries</a>. Its rebellions, above all the victory at the Battle of the Golden Spurs, showed that town militias on foot could defeat mounted knights, a lesson repeated by the Scots and Swiss in the following decades. Flanders was a hinge of politics between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>: English wool exports and Flemish alliances shaped the opening of the Hundred Years’ War, and Edward III was proclaimed king of France at Ghent in 1340. Under the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Flanders became the core of a rich new state, and its painters, the Flemish Primitives, transformed European art; Jan van Eyck worked in Bruges, and the Ghent Altarpiece was completed in 1432. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> was born in Ghent in 1500. The county’s legacy also lies in its name. The northern, Dutch-speaking part of the old county became the core of the modern region of Flanders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#belgium">Belgium</a>, whose name came to cover all of Dutch-speaking Belgium, while French Flanders around Lille and Dunkirk remains part of France.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#dutch-revolt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt.jpg" alt="Dutch Revolt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Gibraltar_1607.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cornelis Claesz van Wieringen / Formerly attributed to Hendrick Cornelisz Vroom, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The independent county ended with the extinction of the male line of the house of Dampierre. In 1369 Margaret of Male, heiress of Count Louis of Male, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a> and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, and on Louis’s death in 1384 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a> became part of the Burgundian State. Philip and his successors crushed later urban revolts, most bloodily when Ghent was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> at Gavere in 1453. Through the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> to Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a> in 1477, Flanders passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and became one of the provinces of the Habsburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and then of the Spanish Netherlands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> split it: Ghent was a Calvinist republic from 1577 until its recapture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> in 1584, and the strip south of the Scheldt estuary, Zeelandic Flanders, became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, confirmed in 1648. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of France conquered the southern part in a series of wars, gaining Lille in 1668 and other towns by the Treaty of Nijmegen in 1678, while Dunkirk was bought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> in 1662. The remaining county passed in 1714 to the Austrian Netherlands. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brabant-revolution">Brabant Revolution</a> of 1789–1790, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-revolutionary-occupation-of-the-netherlands">French revolutionary armies</a> occupied the region, and on 1 October 1795 the county was annexed by France and divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#departement">departments</a> of Lys and Escaut.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a> was one of the two great manufacturing centres of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, whose cloth towns pioneered urban self-government, defeated French knights at Courtrai in 1302, financed the Burgundian state and nurtured the Flemish Primitives, leaving its name to the modern region of Flanders.</em></p>

